<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Everything Xcode can derive from build settings stays in the build
	     settings; GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE merges those keys on top of this
	     file. So this file holds only the keys that have no INFOPLIST_KEY_
	     counterpart.

	     Export compliance is the counter-example and deliberately not here:
	     ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption does have a counterpart, and it is set to
	     NO as INFOPLIST_KEY_ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption on the Fuel target.
	     Xcode types that setting as a Boolean, so the generated key is a real
	     <false/> rather than the string "NO" an unknown key would produce. Fuel
	     talks to a model provider over HTTPS and adds no encryption of its own,
	     which is what makes the answer NO; without the key App Store Connect
	     asks the same question by hand on every upload. -->

	<!-- Both strings are shown by iOS verbatim, so they say where the picture
	     goes. Fuel sends it to the user's own provider with the user's own key
	     and keeps no copy anywhere but the log entry — the permission sheet is
	     the one place a user asks that question, so it is answered there. -->
	<!-- The two typefaces the design draws, both bundled so the app never
	     reaches the network for a face. Plus Jakarta Sans ships as the variable
	     file because the design uses four of its weights (300/400/500/600) and
	     one variable font is smaller than four statics; DM Mono ships as the
	     single static Regular because 400 is the only mono weight drawn.

	     The entries carry no directory component even though the files live in
	     Resources/Fonts: a synchronized resource folder flattens into the
	     bundle root, so Fonts/ is not part of the path at runtime.

	     Both families are SIL Open Font License 1.1; the licence text sits
	     beside each file in Resources/Fonts. -->
	<key>UIAppFonts</key>
	<array>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans[wght].ttf</string>
		<string>DMMono-Regular.ttf</string>
	</array>

	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Fuel uses the camera to photograph a meal. The picture goes straight to the model provider you set up, with your key, and is kept only in the entry it creates.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Fuel reads a photo you pick so it can estimate the meal. The picture goes straight to the model provider you set up, with your key, and is kept only in the entry it creates.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
